      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tiny Tim is Bob Cratchit's son, characterized by his vulnerability and innocence, symbolizing hope and compassion in the story."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is a sickly child in the Cratchit family who participates in the family's Christmas toast but shows little enthusiasm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is a symbol of innocence and kindness, whose essence is cherished by the Cratchit family and serves as a reminder of compassion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is a symbolic character representing innocence and the impact of kindness, associated with Scrooge's transformation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is a vulnerable young boy in the Cratchit family, whose health and future are a significant concern for his father, Bob."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is depicted as a sickly child in the Cratchit family, representing innocence and the tragic consequences of poverty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is referenced as a character associated with Bob Cratchit, suggesting a vulnerable child whose fate impacts Scrooge's actions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is the sickly son of Bob Cratchit, who uses a crutch and offers thoughtful insights, embodying innocence and hope despite his condition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny Tim is the young child of Bob Cratchit, who is cherished by his family and whose health condition is a source of concern and reflection for them."</data>
